course: dictionary
lesson: 4162
title: Many meanings of goal
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word goal has more than one meaning. Context, the words around it, tells you which one is meant. «Then he went in goal for the Year 3s' lunchtime match, dived full length in the mud and saved a penalty.» Saving a penalty means the goal is the net he guards, and he is the goalkeeper.
explain: A goal can also be something you aim for. «Set a goal to read 50 books» means a target you work towards. In a maths word problem, a goal of 100 pages with 60 read means 100 − 60 = 40 pages still to go. Ask yourself: is this a place, a point, or a target?
explain: «Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal» tells you the goal here is the frame and net, not a point. Use the same check every time: swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A goal can be a target you try to reach, like a goal to read 50 books.
fact: In soccer, hockey and netball, a goal is the point you score when the ball goes into the net.
fact: The goal is also the net or posts the ball must go into, and the goalkeeper guards it.
fact: «Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal; unanchored goals can fall and crush children.» If one wobbles, tell an adult.
fact: Useful phrases: score a goal, set a goal, reach a goal, own goal, goal post.
exercise: «Then he went in goal for the lunchtime match, dived in the mud and saved a penalty.» What does «in goal» mean here? | Running around the field all game | Sitting on the bench | Scoring the most points in the match | *Playing as the goalkeeper who guards the net
exercise: Ana's goal is 100 pages and she has read 60. How many more pages does she need? | 6 | *40 | 160 | 60
exercise: Sam says a goal is always a ball in a net. Is he right? | Yes, a goal is always a ball in a net | No, a goal is always a penalty | Yes, a goal is only a rule of the game | *No, a goal can also be a target you work towards
test: «Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal.» What is the goal in this sentence? | A rule for the game | A point scored in a match | *The net and frame the ball goes into | A target to work towards
test: What do we call the point scored when the ball goes into the net? | A safety | A penalty | *A goal | A foul
exam: Which phrase means you finally achieved what you were aiming for? | Goal post | *Reach a goal | Score a penalty | Own goal
